</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>The selection of <math id="A1.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we investigate how to determine <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (defined in Equation <a href="#A1.E12" title="In A.2 The Sensitivity Analysis of k and 𝛼 ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ POME: Post Optimization Model Edit via Muon-style Projection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) using training data. As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ A.3 The selection of 𝛼 ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ POME: Post Optimization Model Edit via Muon-style Projection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, the optimal <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> consistently corresponds to strong training performance. Therefore, we can simplify the selection process by using training performance as a metric to determine <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T14" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T14.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
